  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1042 PM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nWidely scattered showers and thunderstorms are erupting over El\nPaso and Juarez currently, significantly delayed from what \nseveral runs of the HRRR had been insisting on. The appear to be\ndeveloping in response to very weak outflow boundary convergence.\nThe risk of localized heavy rainfall will last for a couple hours\nover the metro area before shifting east. Radar shows weak outflow\napproaching EPZ from the SW, which could increase coverage over\nELP in the next hour. \n\nOtherwise, tomorrow and Monday continue to look like down days as\ndrier air in the low levels filters in from the NW, and weak\nridging develops over the area. Surface dewpoints look set to tank\ninto the 30s by Monday afternoon, though the fringes of some\nmoisture may impinge on the Bootheel late Monday.\n\nA closed upper low moving into southern California Monday will\nweaken into an open trough Tuesday. Meanwhile, the subtropical\nridge will stay strong over east Texas, causing the whole pattern\nto amplify. SW winds aloft will back around to more of a SSW-S\ndirection and begin advecting copious moisture into the region\nTuesday afternoon/evening, along with convectively-induced \nshortwave troughs rolling up from the Sierra Madres. At the same\ntime, a sprawling surface high over Ontario will build south and\nwest over the Plains. While not quite a backdoor front, increasing\nSE low level winds will pull plenty of low level moisture in from\nthe Gulf of Mexico. With moisture below and above, PWAT values\nwill approach 1.50 inches across much of the CWA by Wednesday\nmorning. \n\nThe 500 mb pattern is also very Fall-like, with much cooler\ntemperatures aloft despite the subtropical tap. High aloft, the\nupper trough takes on a negative tilt configuration, with strong\nupper diffluence across SE Arizona, NM, and far west Texas. This\noverall pattern basically stays in place through Thursday night,\nwith widespread showers and thunderstorms, and likely highly-\nefficient warm-rain processes dominating. The rain will mostly be\nmodulated by the timing of upper level disturbances rolling up\nfrom the south, but the forcing is strong enough to keep rain\nchances going through the night time hours. So, Tuesday will\nprobably start off with the usual diurnal trend for convection\ninitiation, but storms look to persist well through the night.\nSteering flow will favor S to N training of storms, and the\nhighest precip amounts will be determined by where precisely \nthese patterns set up. Wednesday and Thursday also look plenty \nmoist with additional forcing, but convection will be impacted by \nleftover cloud cover. The 00Z GFS is trying to shift the axis for \nheavy precipitation a little further east Wednesday Night and \nThursday Night. This would still put the Sacramento Mountains and \neven ELP in the heaviest rain axis, but start to ease up on the \nGila, if the shift holds. There is still plenty of time for models\nto shift back and forth between now and then.",
